As an irreplaceable feedstock, ammonia has received considerable attention because of its various applications, such as in fertilizers14 and pharmaceuticals,15 and as a refrigerant16 and fuel.17 As a result, 150 million tons of ammonia are produced annually on the global scale18 for use as a raw material in the manufacturing industry. In addition, liquid ammonia is used as a unique solvent because it closely approaches water in the ability to dissolve salt-like substances; it impressively dissolves alkali metals and alkali earth metals forming a conductive solution without appreciable reaction taking place.19 In particular, ammonia is promising as a next protonconducting medium because it has a molecular system most similar to that of water, specically three proton donor sites and one acceptor site with a tetrahedral geometry, a degenerate conjugate acid–base system $\left(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}+\mathrm{NH_{3}}\Leftrightarrow\mathrm{NH_{3}}+\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\right)$ and a small molecular size (kinetic diameter $2.60\mathring{\mathbf{A}}_{.}^{\cdot}$ . Nevertheless, liquid $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ shows a low proton conductivity $\left(10^{-11}\Omega^{-1}\right)$ without an acid source because of its low auto-ionization constant (2.2 $\times\ {10}^{-28}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ .20–23 However, liquid $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{3}}$ involving $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ reveals a molar conductivity of $142~\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , which is slightly lower than that of ${\bf H}_{3}{\bf O}^{+}$ in water $\left(350\ \mathrm{S\cm^{2}\ m o l^{-1}}\right)$ ).21 The handling of $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{3}}$ is also advantageous because it is easy to liquify (at ambient temperature and ca. 8 atm or at $240\ \mathrm{K}$ and ambient pressure), and the infrastructures for $\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{3}}$ technology, such as the production, storage, and transport systems, are well established. However, the corrosive gas phase of $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{3}}$ is a drawback as a conducting medium. Thus, the proton conductivity of $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{3}}$ was typically investigated below the boiling point $(<240\ \mathrm{\K})$ . Therefore, for the practical use of ${\bf N H}_{3}$ as a conducting medium, efficient and robust adsorbents are required as a template for encapsulation of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ species. Metal– organic frameworks (MOFs) or porous coordination polymers24–27 constructed from metal ions and organic ligands have been of interest in a new class of solid-state proton conductors because of their high crystallinity, chemical tunability, considerable porosity, and versatile adsorption ability. Kanda et al. reported, for the rst time, a proton-conductive $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ -type MOF in 1979.28 Since then, noticeable progress has been achieved in this eld.13,29–31 In most cases, proton conductive MOFs have been designed with intrinsic or extrinsic proton sources (Fig. 1). The intrinsic proton sources can be classied by their position. One such source is located in pores as counterions, such as ${\bf{H}}_{3}{\bf{O}}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{Me})_{2}^{+}$ , ${\bf H}_{2}{\bf P}{\bf O}_{4}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{\o{OH}^{-}}$ that are incorporated in the pore during the MOF synthesis to maintain the neutrality of the framework.32–35 Another source is positioned in the organic strut of frameworks as dangling acid functional groups $\mathrm{(-(SO_{3}H)}$ , –(COOH), and – $-\big(\mathrm{PO}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{2}\big)\big)$ , which are not coordinated to the metal center.36–39 This can provide additional protons to the pore environment. The intrinsic sources form H-bonding networks with the framework and additional water molecules, resulting in improved proton conductivity. The extrinsic sources mean the articial encapsulation of protic organic molecules (imidazole and histamine) or charge-neutral nonvolatile acids $\mathrm{\bf{\tilde{H}}}_{2}\mathrm{\bf{SO}}_{4}$ and ${\bf\cal H}_{3}{\bf{P O}}_{4}^{\dagger}.$ ) in the pores of MOFs through a simple inclusion method.40–43 The pores are lled up by protic guest molecules with the formation of the H-bonding network. As a result, the low $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ value of guest molecules can provide a potent high-proton donation ability at moderate temperatures.
In general, the ionic conductivity $(\sigma)$ of MOFs is estimated by the alternating current (AC) impedance measurement of a pelletized microcrystal powder or single crystal in a wide frequency range. The ionic conductivity can be expressed by eqn (1), where $z,e,n$ , and $\mu$ mean the valence of the ion, elementary charge, charge carrier concentration, and carrier mobility, respectively. In addition, the proton conduction mechanisms are evaluated through the relationship of conductivity and temperature in eqn (2), where A, $k_{\mathrm{B}},$ and $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ are the pre-exponential factor, Boltzmann constant, and activation energy, respectively.
$$
\sigma=z e n\mu
$$
$$
\sigma T=A\exp(-E_{\mathrm{a}}/k_{\mathrm{B}}T)
$$
Considering eqn (1), proton conductivity can be enhanced by the control of charge carrier concentration and mobility with an improved conduction pathway. Herein, we examine the selective examples of water (hydronium or hydroxide)- and ammonia (ammonium)-mediated degenerate H-bond systems in MOFs. In particular, their design, conduction properties, and diffusion mechanisms are discussed.
## 2. Water-based degenerate system for proton conduction in MOFs
## 2.1. Additional protons with water molecules
A water-based degenerate system consisting of ${\bf H}_{3}{\bf O}^{+}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ $\left(\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{O}^{+}–\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\right.$ system), where these species are connected through H bonds, is important for showing high proton conductivity. Efficient proton transport using the ${\bf H}_{3}{\bf O}^{+}{\bf-H}_{2}{\bf O}$ system can be described by a combination of a proton jump from ${\bf H}_{3}{\bf O}^{+}$ to a neighboring $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and molecular rotations, and is named the Grotthuss mechanism.11 Kreuer et al. reported a more detailed conduction mechanism using molecular dynamics (MD) simulations,44 showing that the additional proton in the ${\bf H}_{3}{\bf O}^{+}{\bf-H}_{2}{\bf O}$ system migrates through a recombination of two species of ${\mathrm{H}}_{5}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}^{+}$ (Zundel ion) and $\mathrm{{H_{9}O_{4}}^{+}}$ (Eigen ion) through a proton jump within an H bond. The efficient transportation in ${\bf H}_{3}{\bf O}^{\dagger}{\bf\cdot}$ – $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ is also thought to be the origin of the abnormally high mobility of the proton $\left(\mathrm{H}^{+}\colon36.2\left(\times10^{-8}\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~s}^{-1}\mathrm{~V}^{-1}\right)\right)$ in water solvent compared with other ions $(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\colon~4.0$ and $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ : 5.2 $(\times10^{-8}\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~s}^{-1}\mathrm{~V}^{-1}))$ .45 Solid materials showing high proton conductivity at ambient temperature usually have the $\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{O}^{+}-$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ system, as in the case of Naon.46 However, the structure of the inner H-bonding system in such amorphous organic materials could not be visualized. By contrast, MOFs were recently studied as a platform for visualization of the H-bonding system in a solid state because of its highly crystalline properties. We present an overview of the structure and proton conductivity of water and additional protons, i.e., the ${\bf H}_{3}{\bf O}^{+}{\bf-H}_{2}{\bf O}$ system, included in MOFs. An oxalate-bridged two-dimensional (2-D) MOF, $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{2}(\mathrm{H_{2}a d p})$ $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{a}\mathbf{d}\mathbf{p}\right.=$ adipic acid), showing the coexistence of a carboxylic acid group and a water molecule in the interlayer space, was reported in 2009.47 As shown in Fig. 2, the carboxylic acidic groups, water molecules, and ammonium ions formed 2-D H-bonding networks, where the dicarboxylic acid group of $\mathrm{\bfH}_{2}\mathrm{adp}$ is expected to give additional protons through partial dissociation. There are several short H bonds between $\mathrm{\bfH}_{2}\mathrm{\bfadp}$ and ox ions $(2.688(4)\mathring{\mathbf{A}})$ ,
O(10), and O(9) atoms of water molecules $\left(2.638{\left(12\right)}{\mathring\mathrm{A}}\right)$ , and $\mathbf{N}(1)$ atoms of ammonium ions and O(10) atoms of water molecules (2.789(7) A˚), implying that the additional protons should momentarily form the ${\bf H}_{3}{\bf O}^{+}{\bf-H}_{2}{\bf O}$ system in the pores and could migrate through the Grotthuss mechanism. This compound shows a high proton conductivity of $0.8\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ $(25\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C},$ $98\%$ relative humidity (RH)), which is comparable to that of Naon. From the results of the temperature dependence of proton conductivity, the activation energy was estimated to be $0.63\:\mathrm{eV}$ , whereas the conduction mechanism in this compound was expected to be the Grotthuss mechanism because of its closely packed guest molecules. The reason for the relatively high activation energy might be attributed to some direct diffusion process in the proton transfer in this compound, which is implied by the $50\%$ occupancy of O(10) atoms with a remarkably short distance between O(10) and ${\mathrm{O}}(10)^{\prime}$ (2.189 A˚). The crystalline character of MOFs gave us the opportunity to discuss such a relationship between the structure of the H-bonding network and additional acid groups in the solid.
This compound also shows two different hydrated states of dihydrate $(10-90\%)$ and anhydrate $\left(0\%\right)$ in addition to the trihydrate state described above.48 The crystal structures of the other states were determined using single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD) analysis (Fig. 3). The dihydrate and anhydrate states showed different space groups of $P2_{1}/c$ (trihydrate: $\bar{P1}$ ) because of a change in the orientation of $\mathrm{\bfH}_{2}\mathrm{adp}$ molecules by tilting in the opposite direction, whereas the fundamental structure was the same as that of the trihydrate (Fig. 4). There is no structural difference between the dihydrate and anhydrate states except for the absence of water molecules. The H-bonding networks of these phases are shown in Fig. 4. The 2-D Hbonding network between ammonium ions, water molecules, and the carboxylic acid group in the trihydrate was changed into a localized H bond in the anhydrate state because of dehydration. In the dihydrate, there also exists a 2-D H-bonding network. However, the averaged distances of the H bonds in the dihydrate became longer than those in the trihydrate. Additionally, the number of H bonds also decreased. These different states showed different conductivities. The anhydrate exhibited an insulating character $\left(\sim10^{-12}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}\right.$ , $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . The conductivity gradually increases with increasing RH $(\leq95\%)$ to show a moderately high proton conductivity of around ${\sim}10^{-4}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ in the dihydrate state. Finally, the trihydrate showed a superprotonic conductivity of around $10^{-2}~\mathrm{S}~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $98\%$ RH. The proton conductivity in this system was consecutively controlled over the range from ${{10}^{-12}}$ to $10^{-2}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ because of the change in the H-bonding networks in the pores.48
Another example of an oxalate-bridged MOF including a carboxylic acid group and water molecules, $\mathrm{\{NR_{3}(C H_{2}C O O H)\}-}$ $\big[\mathbf{M}_{\mathbf{a}}\mathbf{M}_{\mathbf{b}}\big(\mathbf{ox}\big)_{3}\big]\cdot n\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ ( $\mathbf{\tilde{R}}=\mathbf{Et}$ (ethyl) or Bu ( $\overset{\cdot}{n}$ -butyl), $\mathbf{M_{a}M_{b}}=\mathbf{MnCr}$ , FeCr, or FeFe, abbreviated as $\mathbf{R-M_{a}M_{b}})$ was reported by ¯Okawaet al. in 2012 (Fig. 5).49 On the cationic components, the carboxylic acid group was located in the interlayer space to give additional protons. Adsorption measurements revealed that the water molecules $\stackrel{\prime}{n}=0.5{-4}$ , depending on the hydrophilicity of the cationic component) were also present in the pores of the MOF (Fig. 5), but the positions of the water molecules were not determined using SCXRD. The effect of the hydrophilicity of the cationic components on the proton conduction properties was systematically studied. The proton conductivity completely depended on the type of cation (Fig. 6). According to the adsorption measurements, the hydrophilic compounds, i.e., those with a large number of water molecules in the interlayer space, showed a higher proton conductivity than the others. The most hydrophilic compound, Et–FeCr, showed the highest proton conductivity above $10^{-4}~\mathrm{{S}~c m^{-1}}$ $25~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ , $80\%$ RH). By contrast, the hydrophobic compound, Bu–FeCr, does not show high proton conduction under the same conditions. The relationship between water adsorption and proton conductivity in the same framework structure clearly suggested that the H-bonding network in the pore plays a critical role for high proton conduction.
Shigematsu et al. reported on the proton conductivity of MIL53 derivatives, $\mathbf{[M(OH)(bdc-R)]}$ $\mathbf{\Delta}^{\mathbf{\tilde{M}}^{3+}}=\mathbf{Al}^{3+}$ or $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ; $\mathbf{bdc}^{2-}=1\mathbf{,}4\mathbf{\cdot}$ - benzenedicarboxylate; $-\mathbf{R}=-\mathbf{COOH}$ , $-\mathrm{H}$ , $\boldsymbol{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , –OH), having different functional groups such as carboxylic acid, amino, and hydroxyl groups.37 Other derivatives of the 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid (bdc) ligands, having various functional groups, can be introduced without major structural changes of the MIL53 framework. These functional groups are arranged along the one-dimensional (1-D) channels of MIL-53, which can include water molecules (Fig. 7). Then, in the 1-D channel, an additional proton from the acidic functional group, such as –COOH, can be accepted by the included water molecules to form the ${\bf{H}}_{3}{\bf{O}}^{\dagger}-$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ system in the pores. These compounds show proton conductivity in range of $10^{-8}–10^{-5}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ under high humidity $(95\%$ RH) at ambient temperatures (298–353 K). The conductivity completely depends on the functional groups. The carboxylic acid-functionalized MIL-53 showed the highest conductivity, around $10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ , suggesting that the concentration of the additional protons in the pores depends on the $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ of the functional groups. The activation energies of these compounds are estimated to be $0.47\mathrm{-}0.21\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which suggests that the proton conduction in these compounds mainly occurs through the Grotthuss-type mechanism.
The sulfonic acid group is one of the most acidic functional groups, having a relatively low $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ of less than 0, meaning that if water molecules coexist, ${\bf{H}}_{3}{\bf{O}}^{+}$ ions are stoichiometrically formed from the sulfonic acid group. Because the concentration of additional protons in ${\bf H}_{3}{\bf O}^{+}{\bf-H}_{2}{\bf O}$ is directly related to proton conductivity, the introduction of sulfonic acid is an ideal way to increase proton conductivity compared with a weak acid such as carboxylic or phosphonic acid. Phang et al. reported the introduction of the sulfonic acid group into the pores of a UiO-66 framework in 2015 (Fig. 8).36 A post-synthetic method was used to obtain the sulfonic acid-functionalized UiO-66 (UiO-66– $(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathbf{H})_{2},$ ). Oxidation of thiol-functionalized UiO-66 (UiO-66– $(\mathrm{SH})_{2})$ was performed with $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ under hydrothermal conditions to give UiO-66– $(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathbf{H})_{2}$ . Note that UiO-66 and its derivatives are quite stable even under highly acidic conditions.50 $\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ shows a moderate proton conductivity of $2.5~\times$ ${{10}^{-5}}$ S $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . However, aer the post-synthetic reaction, UiO-66– $(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H})_{2}$ shows a superprotonic conductivity of $0.84\ \times\$ $10^{-1}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ under humidied conditions $(90\%$ RH), which is much higher than that of other proton conductors having carboxylic acid $(\sim10^{-3}\mathrm{~s~cm}^{-1})$ and phosphoric acid groups $(\sim10^{-2}~\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{~cm}^{-1})$ ). The activation energy of UiO-66– $(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathbf{H})_{2}$ is estimated to be $0.32\ \mathrm{eV}$ , suggesting the existence of the Grotthuss-type mechanism in this system, while the exact positions of the adsorbed water molecules were not determined.
## 2.2. Hydroxide ions with water molecules
The hydroxide ion $\left(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\right)$ also has potential for constructing a degenerate system with water molecules (the $\mathrm{OH}^{-}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ system) because of the capability of proton “back” transfer from $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ to $\mathrm{\o{OH}^{-}}$ for the migration of the $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ionic carrier. In fact, the existence of the $\mathbf{OH}^{-}{-}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ degenerate system in the water solvent was revealed by Tuckerman et al. in 2002 using MD simulation (Fig. 9).51 The $\mathrm{\o{OH}^{-}}$ ion shows abnormally high mobility in water $\left(20.6{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~s}}^{-1}{\mathrm{~V}}^{-1}\right)$ ,45 which is slightly lower than that of a proton $\left(\mathrm{H}^{+}\colon36.2\right)$ and much higher than that of other ions (e.g., ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ : 4.0), meaning that the $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ion carrier potentially shows high ionic conductivity with water molecules. In the eld of MOFs, $\mathrm{\o{OH}^{-}}$ ion inclusion was believed to be difficult because of the corrosive nature of the $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ions. Thus, MOFs seemed not to be good host materials for $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ions. However, recently, $\mathrm{\o{OH}^{-}}$ ions were successfully incorporated using some alkaline-stable MOFs, and their conductivity has been studied.52,56
In 2014, Sadakiyo et al. reported a basic design of OH ionconductive MOFs. They proposed different types of MOFs that include the $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ion. Type A introduces the $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ions using cationic frameworks, while type B introduces hydroxide salts into the pores. They demonstrated a type B hydroxide ion-conducting MOF, $\left(\mathrm{NBu_{4}}\right)_{m}(\mathrm{A})_{n}\{\mathrm{Zn(mim)_{2}}\}_{6}$ $\mathbf{\langleHmim\mid}=\mathbf{\langle}2$ -methylimidazole; $\mathbf{A}=$ anions) (e.g., $\mathbf{OH}^{-}(\mathbf{NBu_{4}}–\mathbf{ZIF}–\mathbf{8}–\mathbf{OH}))$ , synthesized through a salt inclusion reaction using an alkaline-stable MOF (Fig. 10).52 ZIF-8 (ref. 53) was used as a mother framework to incorporate the hydroxide ion salt of $\mathrm{\DeltaNBu_{4}O H}$ into the pore of the MOF. $\mathbf{NBu_{4}}$ -ZIF-8-OH has an ionic conductivity of $2.3\times10^{-8}$ $\mathrm{{scm}^{-1}}$ ( $99\%$ RH, $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , with $10^{-12}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ for the blank ZIF-8. From water adsorption measurements, the conductivity of $\mathbf{NBu_{4}}$ -ZIF-8-OH is derived from both $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ , suggesting the existence of an ${\bf O H}^{-}{\bf-H}_{2}{\bf O}$ system in the pore. The activation energy was estimated to be $0.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which is much higher than that of hydrated $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ions $(<0.2\ \mathrm{eV})$ 54 and might be attributed to the small apertures in ZIF-8. A type A hydroxide-ion conductor was also reported by Nagarkar et al. in 2016.55 They reported a cationic MOF $\mathrm{\Delta[Ni_{2}(m{\cdot}p y m c a)_{3}]O H{\cdot}\it{n H_{2}O}}$ that includes $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ions as a counter ion together with water molecules in the pores (Fig. 11). The crystalline O atoms of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ or $\mathrm{\OH^{-}}$ ions in the 1-D channel were determined by SCXRD, showing an H-bonding network between them. Thus, the $\mathrm{OH}^{-}.$ – $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ system should exist in the 1-D channel. This MOF showed a high ionic conductivity of $0.8\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ ( $99\%$ RH, $27\ {}^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ at ambient temperatures. They also showed a large difference in conductivities under $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and ${\bf D}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ , which indicates that a proton transfer process is included in the ionic conduction in this system. The activation energy was estimated to be $0.19\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which is quite a low value as in the case of proton conduction by the Grotthuss mechanism, implying that the $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ion conduction in this system occurs by a Grotthuss-like mechanism, i.e., a proton “back” transfer along the H-bonding network.
Another example was reported by Li et al. in 2017.56 Free $\mathrm{\o{OH}^{-}}$ ions together with various cations (i.e., $\mathbf{NBu}_{4}$ and [EVIm] (EVIm ${}=1$ -ethyl-3-vinylimidazolium)) were incorporated into a chargeneutral MOF (Fig. 12), $[\mathrm{Cu}_{6}(\mathrm{NDI})_{3}]$ \$guest (FJU-66, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{NDI}=2,7\cdot$ 一 bis(3,5-dimethyl)dipyrazol-1,4,5,8-naphthalene-tetracarboxydiimide). FJU-66 showed extremely high stability under alkaline conditions at around $\mathrm{pH}14$ and thus could be used as an alkaline-stable mother framework. The conductivity of FJU-66 with the hydroxide salt completely depends on the included salts and is in the range from ${{10}^{-7}}$ to $10^{-2}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ at $30{}^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ under $95\%$ RH. FJU-66[EVIm]OH showed the highest conductivity of $5.7\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ with a very low activation energy of $0.11\ \mathrm{eV_{:}}$ suggesting that ionic conduction occurs through a Grotthusslike mechanism using the proton back transfer.
## 3. Ammonia-based degenerate system for proton conduction in MOFs
The pore of MOFs is a useful space for the encapsulation of small molecules such as gases, organic molecules, and protonconducting media. In particular, ${\bf N H}_{3}$ adsorption in MOFs has been investigated for the storage of a renewable energy source with a high energy density, as well as removal of corrosive and toxic gases. In general, however, the presence of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ species is detrimental to most MOF compounds because ${\bf N H}_{3}$ can replace the existing ligand, resulting in structural decomposition. Nevertheless, the $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ $\mathrm{(NH_{4}^{+})}$ encapsulated in a pore can serve as an efficient proton conducting medium in MOFs because of its H-bonding ability. In addition, ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ is able to generate a ${\bf N H_{4}}^{+}{-}{\bf O H}^{-}$ system with water molecules because of its high solubility. Meanwhile the degenerate system of ${\bf N H_{4}}^{+}{-}{\bf N H}_{3}$ can be described as a coexistence of protonated $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{3}}$ and pure ${\bf N H}_{3}$ . The mobility of $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ in the presence of $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{3}}$ is $0.94\pm0.35\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ $\mathbf{s}^{-1}\mathbf{V}^{-1}$ ,57 which is a lower value than that for other ions $\left(\mathbf{H}^{+}\right)^{+}$ $\mathrm{\o{oH}^{-}}$ , and $\mathrm{Li}^{+})^{45}$ in water. However, its H-bonding ability facilitates long range proton diffusion through the H-bond network. In this section, we deal with the stability of MOFs against ${\bf N H}_{3}$ and $\mathbf{NH}_{3}$ $\left(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\right)$ -mediated proton conductivity, and the dynamic behavior of $\mathbf{NH}_{3}$ $\mathrm{(NH_{4}^{+})}$ in the pores of MOFs for proton conduction.
## 3.1. Stability of metal–organic frameworks against $\mathbf{NH}_{3}$
The structural stability of MOFs has been widely investigated with respect to thermal, chemical, and mechanical aspects because it is an important factor for various applications.50,51,58 In particular, the stability in the presence of water species is a crucial issue for real-world applications because water vapor exists in process environments such as natural gas streams, industrial ue gas, or pollutant separation.59 Moreover, the stability of MOFs toward hydrolysis is one of the key factors for proton-conductive MOFs. Like in a water system, ${\bf N H}_{3}$ -mediated proton conductivity in MOFs can be affected by structural stability under the ${\bf N H}_{3}$ atmosphere because $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ is able to work not only as a conducting medium, but also as a coordinating ligand. Fundamentally, the structural stability of MOFs against ${\bf N H}_{3}$ is estimated by the gas sorption isotherm and powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern analysis. The unstable MOFs indicate decreased porosity and surface areas with degradation of crystallinity, including peak broadening in PXRD patterns during the repeated ${\bf N H}_{3}$ adsorption–desorption process. Previously, MOFs constructed from $\mathrm{{zn}_{4}O}$ or Cu-paddlewheels as secondary building units (SBUs) have been reported to be unstable in ${\bf N H}_{3}$ .60,61 By contrast, in most cases, there is no mention of structural stability for MOFs in ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ sorption experiments. In 2014, Kajiwara et al. reported on a systematic stability study of various types of MOFs against $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ gas (Table 1).62 They considered the effects of the metal cation species and type of organic linker. To check the stability of MOFs against $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , degassed samples were loaded into an ${\bf N H}_{3}$ exposure system for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ at various temperatures and PXRD patterns were compared before and aer $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ exposure. Some MOFs that had high thermal stability decomposed at room temperature under the ${\bf N H}_{3}$ atmosphere. The metal center of MOFs is strongly related to stability. Specically, the divalent metal cation is unstable under ${\bf N H}_{3}$ , except for MOF-74 and ZIF-8. In addition,
MOFs constructed from oxophilic metal cations, such as $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125\mathrm{(Ti}^{\mathrm{\tiny{IV}}}\big)$ , $\mathrm{UiO(Zr^{IV})}$ , $\mathbf{MIL}{\cdot}103\big(\mathrm{Tb}^{\mathrm{III}}\big).$ and $\mathbf{MOF{\overline{{-}}}}76(\mathrm{Ln}^{\mathrm{III}}).$ show a high stability toward $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , whereas the less oxophilic $\mathrm{In}^{\mathrm{III}}$ center is kinetically labile and reacts easily with the N of ${\bf N H}_{3}$ . The most stable MOFs were derived from $\mathsf{A l}\mathrm{(III)}$ and $\mathrm{{Cr}\mathrm{{(m)}}}$ metal cations because of the inertness of the metal cation and oxophilicity. Furthermore, the ligand length and the metal– ligand bond strength are stability factors. Longer ligands weaken the structural stability because of the high void space generation. For ZIF-8, the anionic mim linker with a formal charge of $-0.5$ on each N atom has a stronger basicity than ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ and is strongly bound to the metal center. Consequently, seven types of MOFs, such as MIL-53(Al), Al-BTB, MOF-76(M) $\mathbf{[M=Y}_{i}$ Yb), MIL-101(Cr), ZIF-8, and MOF-74 $\left(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}\right)$ , which have an oxophilic $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}$ center, chemically inert $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{III}}$ center, chelate-type coordinating ligand, or anionic linker, showed high stability with $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ up to $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . These materials are also highly stable in water,63,64 which implies that the stability of MOFs against ${\bf N H}_{3}$ has a similar trend toward water. Considering the predictions for the deconstruction of MOFs by ${\bf N H}_{3}$ , the decomposition mechanism is similar to that of water.60,65
Rieth and Dinca reported in 2018 on ${\bf N H}_{3}$ adsorption capacity, kinetics, and structural stability against $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{3}}$ for isoreticular MOFs.66 They synthesized $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\mathbf{BTDD}$ and $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}.$ BBTA, which have different pore sizes achieved by ligand extension (Fig. 13, $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}$ , Ni, Cu; $\mathrm{BTDD}=\mathrm{bis}(1H{-}1,2,3$ -triazolo $[4,5-b],[4^{\prime},5^{\prime}-i])$ dibenzo[1,4]dioxin; $\mathbf{BBTA}\ =\ \mathbf{1}H,\mathbf{5}H.$ -benzo(1,2- d),(4,5- $d^{\prime}$ )bistriazole). Among the three metal species, $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ compounds are most stable toward ${\bf N H}_{3}$ . $\mathbf{Ni}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\mathbf{BBTA}$ with a small pore $(13\mathrm{~\AA~})$ is only an example that retains its surface area aer 1 bar of ${\bf N H}_{3}$ treatment, and $\mathbf{Ni}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\mathbf{BTDD}$ with a large pore (23 A˚) withstands pore lling with ${\bf N H}_{3}$ exhibiting a diminished surface area. However, ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ compounds cannot withstand pore lling of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ (1 bar of $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf N\mathbf H_{3}\dot{}.}\end{array}$ ), and both $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ compounds are unstable even at low ${\bf N H}_{3}$ concentrations. This stability trend is attributed to the kinetic metal–aquo substitution rate because $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ is approximately kinetically four orders of magnitude more inert than $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and two orders of magnitude more inert than $\mathbf{CO}^{2+}\left(\mathbf{Ni}^{2+}>\mathbf{CO}^{2+}>\mathbf{Cu}^{2+}\right)$ . As a relevant stability parameter, this type of kinetic stability has been applied in a previous study to explain the robustness of isostructural frameworks such as MIL- $100(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+})$ , MIL-54 $(\mathbb{A}^{1^{3+}})$ , and 47 $(\mathrm{V}^{4^{+}})$ MOFs. The robustness was in the order of $\mathrm{Cr^{3+}>A l^{3+}>V^{4+}}$ .67 According to previous studies, undoubtedly, the structural stability of MOFs toward ${\bf N H}_{3}$ has a strong relationship with their stability in water. Therefore, an increased metal–ligand bond strength provides the chemical robustness of MOFs. One of the strategies for improving stability—although it is synthetically challenging—is the incorporation of kinetically inert and oxophilic metal species into the framework. Future endeavors in this direction are expected to be benecial for producing durable MOFs materials to ${\bf N H}_{3}$ . The next section describes and discusses the proton conductivity of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ $\left(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\right)$ encapsulated in a representative set of MOFs.
## 3.2. $\mathbf{NH}_{3}\ (\mathbf{NH_{4}}^{+})$ as a proton-conducting medium in MOFs
For water molecules, known as the most efficient conducting medium, the conjugate acid $\left(\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{O}^{\dagger}\right)$ and base $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\right)$ play a crucial role in the proton diffusion path. Likewise, the conjugate acid–base of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ is an interesting proton conduction path. In general, the proton conductivity of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ has been investigated at a low temperature $(<\mathsf{b p}\colon240\mathrm{~K~})$ because of the gas phase problem. However, the use of porous frameworks allows ${\bf N H}_{3}$ -mediated conductivity to be studied in a wide temperature range above and below room temperature, where ${\bf N H}_{3}$ can be encapsulated in pores as $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ or $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{3}}$ forms. The major examples of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ -based proton conduction systems reveal ${\bf N H}_{4}^{+}{-\bf H}_{2}{\bf O}{-\bf\Phi}$ -mediated proton conductivity rather than a ${\mathrm{NH_{4}}}^{+}.$ – ${\bf N H}_{3}$ degenerate system. In this section, therefore, the proton conductivity in MOFs through $\mathrm{NH}_{3}\left(\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}\right)$ species is discussed focusing on the incorporation methods of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ $\left(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\right)$ species for proton conductivity.
3.2.1 Encapsulation of $\mathrm{{NH_{4}}^{+}}$ in MOFs as a counterion. For structural neutrality, some MOFs incorporate a counter ion species as a lattice component during the synthetic process.32–35,47,68–70 In particular, protic counterions $\left(\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{O}^{+}\right.$ or $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ ) included in pores for charge compensation can serve as proton carriers. As mentioned above, oxalate-bridged 2-D honeycomb sheets of $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]^{2-}$ , $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]$ \$ $3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{2}\mathbf{adp}=:$ adipic acid) show a superprotonic conductivity of $10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ with $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ species.47
The role of $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ species in proton conductivity has been proven by metal cation substitution (Fig. 14).71 Sadakiyo et al. reported in 2014 on $\mathrm{K}_{2}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp}\big)[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ where the $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ of $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was replaced by $\mathbf{K}^{^{+}}$ without an apparent structural change. For $\mathrm{K}_{2}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp}\big)[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , the H bonds in the ox ion and $\mathrm{\bfH}_{2}\mathrm{adp}$ $(\mathbf{O}(1)^{...}\mathbf{O}(7)$ , 2.678(2) A˚);
between $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{adp}$ and water molecules $(\mathbf{O}(7){\cdots}\mathbf{O}(10)$ , 3.043(4) A˚ and $\mathbf{O}(7){\cdot}{\cdot}{\cdot}\mathbf{O}(9)$ , $3.122(3)\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ ; and between water molecules $(\mathbf{O}(9){\cdot}{\cdot}{\cdot}\mathbf{O}(10)$ , 2.711(7) $\mathring\mathbf{A}\}$ are closed to those of $(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp})$ $\mathrm{\big[Zn}_{2}\mathrm{\big(ox\big)}_{3}\big]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ . This implied that the counterion exchange is not affective in a guest molecule arrangement. However, a remarkable difference is the non-hydrogen bonding of the $\mathbf{K}^{^{+}}$ ion instead of the H-bonding $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ , despite the nearly identical position (Fig. 14). The absence of H-bonding associated with $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ provided a lower proton conductivity $\left(1.2\times10^{-4}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}\right.$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ , under $98\%$ RH) by approximately two orders of magnitude compared with that of $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ . These results strongly suggest that the $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ medium is crucial not only for increasing proton carrier concentration by acidic character $\left(\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}=9.2\right)$ , but also for facilitating proton transfer by forming $\mathbf{H}$ bonding with neighboring molecules.
In 2011, Pardo et al. reported an oxalate-bridged bimetallic 3-D chiral anionic network including $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ species with the formula $\mathrm{\left(NH_{4}\right)_{4}[M n C r_{2}(o x)_{6}]\cdot4H_{2}O}$ (Fig. 15).72 In the framework, two types of oxalate ligands are observed. One of them shows the uncoordinated O of the oxalate ligand, which is directed toward ordered water molecules in channel A along the helical $c$ -axis. Another oxalate ligand links $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{III})}}$ to $\mathbf{Mg(\mathrm{{II})}}$ ions in a bisbidentate way. The counterions $\mathrm{(NH_{4}^{+})}$ and the guest molecules $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\right)$ are located around the oxalate network in the A channel, giving rise to high proton conductivity at room temperature. In the two types of channels (A and B), the A channel, decorated by the terminal O of oxalate, has a well-dened helical chain of ammonium cations $[\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}^{...}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}=2.839(7)-2.892(6)\mathring\mathrm{A}].$ , indicating Hbonding networks with the $^{\mathrm{~\small~O~}}$ atoms of the terminal oxalates $[\mathrm{N}{\cdots}0~=~2.795(6)\ –2.960(8)~\mathring{\mathrm{A}}]$ . Although water molecules are present in both pores, the water molecules in channel A, stabilized by the terminal oxalate O with an H-bonding network, are only rened. As humidity increases, the $\sigma$ value is enhanced by ve orders of magnitude from $2.4\times10^{-9}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ at $9\%$ RH to $1.1\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ at $96\%$ RH, which improved to $1.7\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $313\mathrm{~K~}$ The activation energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ for the ionic conductivity estimated from the Arrhenius plot is 0.23 eV (Fig. 15b inset). These results imply that the high proton conductivity is derived from an efficient H-bonding network through the Grotthuss mechanism. They believed that the A channel that included H-bonding networks among the terminal oxalate, $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ cations, and ordered $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules is an attractive potential pathway for the proton conduction triggered by humidity.
3.2.2 Aqueous- $\mathbf{NH}_{3}$ inclusion in MOFs. Aqueous ${\bf N H}_{3}$ (ammonia water) is a solution of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ in water. Recently, a few results have been reported for the proton conductivity of MOFs using various concentrations of aqueous $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{3}}$ solution vapor instead of pure water vapor.73–75 In 2014, Bazaga-Garc´ıa et al. reported the enhanced proton conductivity of a calcium phosphonate framework through post-synthetic modications using aqueous $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ .73 The calcium phosphonate framework was constructed from ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ ions and the rigid polyfunctional ligand 5-(dihydroxyphosphoryl) isophthalic acid (PiPhtA), giving the formula $\mathrm{Ca_{2}[(H O_{3}P C_{6}H_{3}C O O H)_{2}]_{2}[(H O_{3}P C_{6}H_{3}(C O O)_{2}H)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}$ \$ ${\mathrm{5H}}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ , Ca-PiPhtA-I. Ca-PiPhtA-I has a high water content in the ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ metal centre (two water molecules coordinated) and a 1-D channel (ve lattice water molecules). In this pillared layered structure, the hydrophilic 1-D channel is observed along the $b$ -axis, which is lled with ve lattice water molecules per unit cell. The lattice water molecules exhibit water-to-water interactions providing an extended H-bond network along the 1-D channels as a potential proton transfer pathway. The removal of the ve lattice water molecules at ${75-130\ ^{\circ}}\mathbf{C}$ indicated a partially dehydrated structure (Ca-PiPhtA-II, Fig. 16b). To include the $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ species in the pore, Ca-PiPhtA-I was exposed to $28\%$ aqueous $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ solution, resulting in the inclusion of $\mathsf{7N H}_{3}$ and $16\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules in Ca-PiPhtA- ${\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{3}}$ . However, a detailed structural analysis of Ca-PiPhtA- ${\bf N H}_{3}$ is impossible because of the lack of crystallinity with a partial structure decomposition. The IR spectrum of Ca-PiPhtA- $\mathbf{NH}_{3}$ reveals that the water stretching vibrational signal shis to a lower wave number, implying the presence of a strong $\mathbf{H}$ bond. Interestingly, the dehydrated sample does not take up $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ in $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ -temperature programmed desorption (TPD) measurements, which means that ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}–\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ adsorption occurred through the cooperative mechanism of Hbonding interactions. The proton conductivity values for CaPiPhtA-I and Ca-PiPhtA-II were $5.7\times{{10}^{-4}}$ and $3.6\times10^{-4}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ at $24~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ and $98\%$ RH, respectively. These values increased up to $1.3\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ at $40~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ (water-equilibrated state was called Ca-PiPhtA-III). However, the conductivity value was not retained at high temperatures $(>45^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ because of the dehydration process. Ca-PiPhtA- ${\bf\cdot N H}_{3}$ represented a maximum value of $\sigma$ (6.6 $\times10^{-3}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1})$ at $24^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ and $98\%$ RH. The activation energies $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ for the proton transfer in Ca-PiPhtA-I $(0.32~\mathrm{eV})$ , Ca-PiPhtA-III $(0.23\ \mathrm{eV})$ , and Ca-PiPhtA- ${\bf\cdot N H}_{3}$ $\ensuremath{\left(0.40\mathrm{eV}\right)}$ are in the range of those of the Grotthuss mechanism.
Liang et al. investigated in 2014 the difference in proton conductivity in water and various concentrations of the aqua $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ medium. They used two 3-D $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ MOFs, $\mathrm{\{[Co_{3}(m{-}C l P h I D C)_{2}(H_{2}{-}}}$ $\left.\mathrm{\mathbf{O}}\right)_{6}]$ \$ $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big\}_{n}$ [1; $m{\mathrm{-ClPhH}}_{3}{\mathrm{IDC}}=2$ -( $m$ -chlorophenyl)imidazole-4,5- dicarboxylic acid] and $\{\mathrm{[CO_{3}(}p\mathrm{-ClPhHIDC)_{3}(}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{n}$ (2; $\begin{array}{r l r}{p\mathrm{-ClPhH_{3}I D C}}&{{}=}&{2\cdot(\gamma}\end{array}$ $\dot{p}$ -chlorophenyl)imidazole-4,5-dicarboxylic acid) (Fig. 17).74 The two compounds have a similar 1-D channel system and different water contents (coordinating six and two free water molecules for 1 and coordinating three and six free water molecules for 2). Furthermore, these compounds are highly stable while soaking in water and in boiling water, or under treatment with 7.4 M ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}\cdot{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ solution vapor without structural change. However, the crystal structures and guest composition of the aqueous ${\bf N H}_{3}$ -treated samples were not given. The conductivity of compound 1 under humid conditions $98\%$ RH, $30{}^{\circ}\mathbf{C}_{\mathcal{I}}^{\mathsf{{\rangle}}}$ was $2.73\times$ $10^{-6}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ and increased to $7.62\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with an activation barrier of $1.58\:\mathrm{eV}$ By contrast, compound 2 showed a maximum proton conductivity of $2.47\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ at $90~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ and $98\%$ RH. For further enhanced proton conductivity, the two MOFs were exposed to different concentrations of aqua- ${\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{3}}$ vapor from 2.11 to $7.40\mathrm{{M}}$ As the $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ concentration increased to $7.40\mathrm{{M}}$ , the conductivity values of the two MOFs increased by two orders of magnitude, from $7.44\times10^{-7}$ to $4.39\times10^{-5}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ for 1 and from $5.71\times{{10}^{-7}}$ to $4.23\times10^{-5}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ for 2. At $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a concentration of $7.4\mathrm{\bf~M}$ , these MOFs have a superionic conductivity of $2.89\times10^{-2}$ and $4.25\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. Obviously, both factors, a high concentration of aqua ${\bf N H}_{3}$ vapor and temperature, are benecial for proton conduction. The activation energy barrier of compound 1 at an ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}–\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ concentration of $\mathbf{4.93\:M}$ was changed from $0.25\mathrm{eV}$ at $30{-}60\ {^{\circ}}\mathbf{C}$ to 0.73 at ${\bf70-100}^{\circ}{\bf C},$ which means that proton-hopping diffusion is favorable through the H-bonding networks at low temperatures $({<}60~^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ , and that the vehicular transfer motion of $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ and ${\bf{H}}_{3}{\bf{O}}^{+}$ is dominant at high temperatures $({>}60^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ . The enhanced conductivity triggered by the aqueous– ${\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{3}}$ vapor originated from protonation of the conducting medium and the H-bonding ability of ${\mathrm{NH_{4}}}^{+}$ with a synergistic effect on the proton conduction process. However, for an intuitive understanding of the proton conduction mechanism, obtaining a convincing structure for $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ conned in MOFs remains challenging.
3.2.3 Additional protonation to anhydrous $\mathbf{NH}_{3}$ in MOFs. Previous studies have demonstrated $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ $\left(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\right)$ -mediated proton conductors through the simultaneous introduction of the $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ counterion during MOF synthesis or aqueous- ${\bf\cdot N H}_{3}$ vapor loading exhibiting an improved proton conductivity. Anhydrous $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ -mediated proton conductivity was reported recently using MIL-53(Al) derivative MOFs through pure ${\bf N H}_{3}$ gas adsorption.76 MIL-53 (Al) derivatives that are well known for their high stability are synthesized from ${\bf A}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions and $\mathtt{H}_{2}\mathtt{b d c}$ ligands with various functional groups, $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}{-}(\mathrm{COOH})_{2}\right.$ , 1), $\mathrm{(H_{2}b d c-(N H_{2}),~}$ 2), $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}-\left(\mathrm{OH}\right),3\right)$ , and $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}\operatorname{bdc},~4\right)$ , indicating the difference in proton-donating ability $\left(\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ . All samples are stable under $100\ \mathrm{kPa}$ of ${\bf N H}_{3}$ . In ${\bf N H}_{3}$ adsorption–desorption measurements, compound 1 adsorbed two more additional equivalents of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ than the other compounds because of the acid–base reaction by the dangling two carboxylic acid groups in the ligand (Fig. 18a). This implies that $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{3}}$ can exist as $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ species, which is conrmed by IR spectroscopy, XPS, and solidstate $^2\mathrm{H}$ NMR. The crystal structures incorporating $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ molecules were determined by Rietveld renement analysis of PXRD, which shows the formation of an H-bonding network among the ${\bf N H}_{3}$ , the framework $\scriptstyle\mathbf{\mu}_{\mathrm{{\mu}}\mathrm{{-}}\mathrm{{OH}}}$ and the functional group of the ligand), and the adjacent $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ (Fig. 18c and f). The conned ${\bf N H}_{3}$ guests are aligned in a zigzag shape along the 1-D channels, and the distance of adjacent ${\bf N H}_{3}$ was shortened with decreased acidity of the functional group because of the reduced attraction from the pore wall. Meanwhile, $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ molecules conned in MIL53(Al)– $\boldsymbol{\cdot}(\mathrm{COOH})_{2}$ are well distributed in the overall framework with a strong interaction supported by ${\bf N H}_{3}$ -TPD. Proton conductivity was measured by using an in situ system with controlled pure ${\bf N H}_{3}$ gas pressure. The initial low conductivity values of the four compounds under vacuum at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ $(1.2\times$ ${{10}^{-12}}$ , $2.8~\times~10^{-13}$ , $1.1~\times~10^{^{-13}}$ , and $6.6~\times~10^{-13}\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1};$ markedly increased up to $4.9\times10^{-7}$ , $1.0\times{{10}^{-9}}$ , $4.2\times{{10}^{-9}}$ , and $9.1\times10^{-11}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ for 1, 2, 3, and 4, respectively, as the $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ pressure increased to $100\mathrm{\kPa}$ (Table 2). The small change in the ${\bf N H}_{3}$ pressure and pore environment by the functional group triggers a large change in the conductivity. As expected, the acid functional group in the ligand strongly affects the proton conductivity and the simultaneous formation of $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ species plays a key role in a degenerate $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}+\mathrm{NH_{3}}\Leftrightarrow\mathrm{NH_{3}}+$ $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ proton diffusion pathway. Under the saturated ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ pressure at $290\mathrm{~K~}$ the conductivity of compound 1 became $2.56\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ , which is comparable to the anhydrous proton conductivity in MOFs. The activation energy for proton conduction in compound 1 estimated by using the Arrhenius plot under ${\bf N H}_{3}$ saturated pressure at various temperatures is $0.46\ \mathrm{eV}.$ . These results are the rst report of anhydrous $\mathrm{NH}_{3}.$ - mediated proton-conductive MOFs with crystallographic analysis.
## 3.3. Dynamic behavior of $\mathrm{{NH_{4}}^{+}}$ for proton conductivity in MOFs
Molecular dynamics (MD) is one of the interesting topics regarding porous MOFs. Molecules located in the pore interact with a framework and exhibit a unique behavior. Indeed, the dynamic behavior of the conducting medium incorporated in porous materials helps in understanding the proton conduction mechanism. In particular, quasi-elastic neutron scattering (QENS) and solid-state NMR are powerful tools for investigating the dynamic molecular motion of protic species because the H atom has a larger incoherent neutron-scattering cross section $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\mathrm{inc}}=80b$ , $1b=10^{-24}\mathrm{cm}^{2}.$ ) compared with other atoms such as C $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\cdot}\sigma_{\mathrm{inc}}=0b$ ), N $[\sigma_{\mathrm{inc}}=0.5b)$ , O $(\sigma_{\mathrm{inc}}=0b)$ , and D $(\sigma_{\mathrm{inc}}=2.1b)$ , and NMR is a selectively sensitive probe for local MD. Therefore, the dynamic motions of all species containing an H atom can be selectively detected through incoherent neutron scattering and NMR.
In 2014, Miyatsu et al. claried the relationship between proton conductivity and the phase transition associated with the dynamic behavior of $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ and water molecules in $2{\cdot}\mathbf{D}$ $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{K}_{2}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp}\big)[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]$ \$ $3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .77 To measure neutron scattering, the methylene H atoms of adipic acid in $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathbf{K}_{2}(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{adp})$ $\mathrm{[Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were deuterated to reduce their contribution to the scattering signal. Therefore, the major signals originated from $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ , $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ , and carboxylic acid. QENS measurements were carried out using three neutron spectrometers (AGNES, IRIS, and HFBS) with different energy resolutions to cover a time range between 0.1 ps and $10\ \mathrm{ns}$ . In the adiabatic calorimetry experiment, $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathbf{K}_{2}(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{adp})$
$\mathrm{[Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ showed different phase transition temperatures at 86 and $^{138\mathrm{~K~}}$ , respectively. Neutron powder scattering showed changes in the Bragg peaks located at $Q=1.60–1.64\mathrm{A}^{-1}$ and $Q=1.67–1.70\mathrm{~A}^{-1}$ at the transition temperatures (Fig. 19). Compared with $\mathrm{K}_{2}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp}\big)[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ $(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp})$ $\mathrm{[Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ indicated a more dramatic change. We note that SCXRD did not provide a signicant structural change between 50 and $100\mathrm{~K~}$ because of the difficulty of detecting H atoms in $\mathbf{X}$ -rays. This means that the phase transition originated from H atoms. Relaxational dynamics were studied by QENS measurements. The dynamic structure factor $S(Q,\omega)$ was tted to the following equations (Fig. 20a):
$$
\begin{array}{c}{{S(Q,\omega)=R(Q,\omega)\otimes\{\hat{\upsilon}(\omega)+\sum L_{\mathrm{i}}(Q,\omega)\}+\mathrm{BG}}}\\ {{L_{\mathrm{i}}(Q,\omega)={\displaystyle{\frac{\left(T(Q)\right)}{\left(\pi\omega^{2}+T^{2}(Q)\right)}}}}}\end{array}
$$
where $L_{\mathrm{i}}(Q,\omega)$ describes the relaxation of $\mathbf{H}$ atoms and ${\cal{T}}(Q)$ is the half-width at half-maximum of the Lorentz function. The relaxation $\boldsymbol{{\cal T}}(Q)$ is independent of $Q$ which means that the motions of H atoms had a local origin. The Arrhenius plot of the relaxation time in Fig. 20b shows four relaxation modes $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}=\right.$ 2.3, 4.5, 7.7, and $12.9\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for $\mathbf{L}_{1},\mathbf{L}_{2},\mathbf{L}_{3}$ , and $\mathbf{L}_{4}$ , respectively) in $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{2}(\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and only two relaxation modes ( $\mathrm{\bar{L}}_{\mathrm{K1}}$ and ${\bf L}_{\mathrm{K}2}\breve{} $ ) in $\mathrm{K}_{2}(\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ The $\mathbf{L}_{\mathbf{K}1}$ and ${\mathbf{L}}_{\mathrm{K}2}$ modes were observed around $\mathbf{L}_{3}$ and $\mathbf{L}_{4}$ , which means that four modes $(\mathrm{L}_{3},\ \mathrm{L}_{4},\ \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{K1}},$ and ${\bf L}_{\mathrm{K}2}\breve{}$ ) were derived from water molecules, whereas the $\mathbf{L_{1}}$ and $\mathbf{L}_{2}$ motions having a low activation energy are associated with $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ . The $\mathbf{H}$ bonds of $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ were weaker than that of water and indicated low activation energy. The geometrical information of the two relaxation modes for $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ , $\mathbf{L}_{1}$ , and $\mathbf{L}_{2}$ was studied through the relative integrated intensities of the elastic incoherent structure factor (EISF) (Fig. 20c). The incoherent scattering reects only the H atom motion in $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ with a tetrahedral geometry because of the negligible incoherent scattering of the $\mathbf{N}$ atom. Among the several dynamic models, such as $C_{3}$ jump, $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ jump, and continuous rotation about the $C_{3}$ axis and $C_{2}$ axis, $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ ions continuously rotate around the $C_{3}$ axis during the jumping motion between two preferred orientations. From these results, the phase transition and unique dynamic motion of $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ for proton conduction could be clearly observed.
The dynamic motion and proton conduction mechanism of $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ conned in MIL-53(Al)– $\scriptstyle\cdot(\mathbf{COOH})_{2}$ were investigated by $^2\mathrm{H}$ solid-state NMR through line shape and spin-relaxation time analysis at various temperatures (Fig. 21).76 As mentioned above, pure ${\bf N H}_{3}$ molecules are converted to $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ by protonation from the dangling $\mathbf{\Gamma}_{-}(\mathbf{COOH})_{2}$ in the ligand. Furthermore, one of the $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ molecules has an interaction with the $\upmu_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{OH}$ bridge. These results are reected in an anisotropic signal (Ia: coupling parameters, $Q_{\mathrm{eff}}=73~\mathrm{kHz}$ , $\eta_{\mathrm{eff}}=0$ ; and Ib: $Q_{0}=223$ $\mathbf{kHz}$ , $\eta=0.1$ , static $\mathrm{ND_{4}}^{+}$ ) and an isotropic one (II, mobile ${\mathrm{ND}}_{4}^{+}$ ). The anisotropic signal exhibits rapid rotational motion of static $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{3}}$ molecules around their $C_{3}$ symmetry axis. In addition, restricted librations between $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{\mu-O-D-N D}}_{3}$ contributed to the larger coupling constant in the anisotropic signal. The isotropic signal originated from the complex isotropic reorientation and local internal rotational motion of mobile $\mathrm{{ND_{4}}^{+}}$ with two distinct rotation mechanisms. All dynamic motions are shown in Fig. 21c. Although the major species of ${\bf N D}_{3}$ conned in the pore are the mobile $\mathrm{ND_{4}}^{+}$ in the overall temperature range, a small fraction of static ${\bf N D}_{3}$ is present even at $313\mathrm{~K~}$ , implying a slow exchange rate between both species $\left(<0.5~\mathrm{\kHz}\right)$ . The equilibrium constant $\left(K_{\mathrm{eq}}\right)$ derived from $\exp(\Delta S/R)$ $\exp(-\Delta H/R T)$ , where $\Delta S=30.34\mathrm{~J~mol}^{-1}\mathrm{~K}^{-1}$ and $\Delta H=3.4\ \mathrm{kJ}$ $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , coincides with the quantitative analysis of a relative population of two species through line-shape analysis. The $T_{1}$ relaxation of the anisotropic signal shows a clear minimum (region a), whereas $T_{1}$ of the isotropic signal exhibits at-like behavior and a drastic increase in the high-temperature region (regions $\mathbf{a}^{\prime}$ and $\mathbf{b}_{\mathbf{\alpha}}^{\prime}$ ). This means that the mobile ${\mathrm{ND}}_{4}^{+}$ has complex multiple motions in isotropic reorientation and an activation process mechanism change with the same geometrical motion. Considering the proton conduction mechanism, the local internal rotational motion of mobile ${\bf N_{\mathrm{{D_{4}}}}}^{+}$ is responsible for the Grotthuss mechanism, resulting in the diffusion coefficient $(D)$ evaluated from $C_{3}$ axis rotation motion being 3.3 $\times~10^{-10}~\mathrm{{m}}^{2}~\mathrm{{s}}^{-1}$ with a $20\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ activation barrier. Furthermore, the high activation energy for proton conduction $(0.46\mathrm{eV})$ estimated from AC impedance measurements can account for the isotropic reorientation motion with random rotations, as well as the jump–diffusion process observed in region $\ensuremath{\mathbf{c}}^{\prime}$ . The activation barrier of the related motion is $50\mathrm{~kJ~mol}^{-1}$ , with a slow correlation time $\left(\tau=3.6\times10^{-5}~\mathrm{s}\right)$ , which gives a low diffusion coefficient $\left(1\times10^{-15}{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~s}}^{-1}\right)$ at $300~\mathrm{K}.$ Although this is a lower diffusion coefficient value than that of the hopping mechanism, the value is acceptable considering the observed conductivity. The solid-state NMR measurement strongly supports the proton conduction mechanism predicted from the crystallographic analysis.
## 3.4. Theoretical/computational study for the proton conduction mechanism
Understanding of the conduction mechanism is a key for rational designing of proton conductors. In general, polymer based-electrolytes have a lack of long range-order resulting in difficulty to characterize their conduction mechanism. However, MOFs are able to provide fundamental insight into the conduction mechanism due to their crystalline properties. Nevertheless, unlike a lot of experimental observations for proton conduction in MOFs, the theoretical and computational studies to elucidate the proton mobility inside the pore are still quite scarce. In 2013, Paesani reported molecular dynamic simulation of proton mobility in water-mediated proton transfer of exible MIL-53(Cr), constructed from $\mathrm{CrO}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ clusters with terephthalate linkers forming 1 D channels.78 They employed the anharmonic multistate empirical valence bond (aMS-EVB)79 model in a 32 unit cell of a framework with a function of the loading number $(N)$ of water molecules $\mathbf{\partial}N=\mathbf{\partial}$ 0 to $20\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ per unit cell and temperature (300, 350, and $400\mathrm{K}$ . The model describes not only a hydrated structure but also a charge defect related to the presence of an excess proton in the H-bond network. The excess proton was randomly added to one $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecule and equilibrated for 1 ns. The radial distribution function (RDF) indicated the relationship between a center of excess charge (CEC) and bridge hydroxy group $\left(\upmu_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH}\right)$ . At a loading of $N\leq10\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , two broad and structured peaks are located at $R=3.0$ and $5.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ which are indicative of strong interaction between the excess proton and $\upmu_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{OH}$ in the framework, whereas a higher $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ loading inducing pore opening by breathing shows fewer structured RDF peaks due to the screening of direct interaction by the solvation of the excess proton with water molecules. In addition, the hydrated proton structure in the pores was determined by analysis of the two dimensional probability distribution $P(\delta,R_{\mathrm{O0O1}})$ which is a difference of distance between the center of the excess charge (CEC) and the adjacent oxygen atoms ( $\mathrm{{\dot{Q}}_{0}}$ and $\begin{array}{r}{{\bf O}_{1}\overline{{\bf\Lambda}}_{.}}\end{array}$ ) of the $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecule $\big(\hat{\partial}=R_{\mathrm{O0-CEC}}-R_{\mathrm{O1-CEC}}\big)$ . According to the analysis, the hydrated proton structure is favorable to form a distorted Eigenlike cation as N increases. In other words, Zundel-like structures of the hydrated proton are present in the narrow pore of MIL-53 with a low $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ content. The transition free energy indicating proton transfer between two adjacent water molecules is expressed as $\Delta F=-k T\ln(q_{\mathrm{reac}}),$ $q_{\mathrm{reac}}={c_{1}}^{2}-{c_{2}}^{2}$ ${\bf\bar{\Phi}}_{c_{1}}{}^{2}$ and ${c_{2}}^{2}$ being the probabilities of the two EVB states) (Fig. 22a). The central point $\zeta_{\mathrm{freac}}=0.0\AA$ means that the excess proton has symmetric hydrogen bonding with donor and acceptor water molecules. As shown in Fig. 22a, the free energy barrier is lower than that of bulk water excluding $N=10$ where MIL-53 undergoes a pore transition from narrow to a large pore. Moreover, the increased temperature reduces the barrier height. Overall, the relatively low free energy barrier clearly reects that the conned environment facilitates more proton conduction than bulk water. The lifetimes, $\tau_{\mathrm{{pc};\mathrm{~}}}$ , evaluated from the tting of the long-time decay of $C_{\mathrm{pc}}(t)$ are short with tens of picoseconds for $N=3$ , 5, 10, which are longer than that of bulk water ( $\dot{\tau}_{\mathrm{pc}}=1.7$ ps).80 It is implied that long distance proton transfer through the conned water in MIL-53 is suppressed compared to that in the bulk system. This result is coincident with the diffusion coefficient (Fig. 22d and e). As a result, the conned water-mediated proton conduction in MIL-53 shows a low free energy barrier for proton transfer with slow proton diffusion suggesting a slow rearrangement of hydrogen bonding in the pore.
Borges et al. reported a joint experimental (QENS)/molecular dynamics (MD) simulation study of water-mediated proton transfer in $\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-(CO_{2}H)_{2}}$ .81 The structure of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{UiO}}\-66(Z1).$ $(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathbf{H})_{2}$ is constructed by $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ cluster connected by six terepthalic acid functionalized with carboxylic acid.. This compound has two types of cages, tetrahedral (5.2 A˚) and octahedral (7.0 A˚) connected by a triangular window. It shows a high conductivity value $\left(2.3\times10^{-3}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}\right)}$ at RH $95\%$ and 90 ${}^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ with a low activation energy of $0.17\ \mathrm{eV}$ . The result from the tting of QENS at low and high $Q$ indicated that there are two dynamic species. One is free protons diffusing to long distance and another is rotating water molecules. Proton diffusion happened predominantly from jumping between the solvated protons through local reorientational motion. The self-diffusion coefficients $\left(D_{\mathrm{s}}\right)$ of the proton and water are $3.5\times10^{-9}\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{s}^{-1}$ and $1.4\times10^{-10}\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ at $373\mathrm{~K~}$ , respectively. Both exhibit lower values than those of bulk water $\left(D_{s}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})=2.3\times10^{-9}\mathrm{m}^{2}\right.$ $\mathbf{s}^{-1}$ and $D_{s}(\mathrm{H}^{+})=9.4\times10^{-9}~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ at $300\ \mathrm{K})^{82,83}$ The computational approach of $D_{\mathrm{s}}$ for water is in good agreement with QENS data, whereas the simulated $D_{\mathrm{s}}({\bf C E C})$ , faster than that of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ , was an underestimated QENS value (Fig. 23a). This observation means that proton transfer is dominated by the Grotthuss mechanism rather than the vehicle mechanism. Interestingly, MD trajectories proved that water molecules are positioned in a tetrahedral cage at $300\mathrm{~K~}$ having a strong interaction with the free $-\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{H}$ functional group. In addition the residence time of water molecules in the tetrahedral cage is quite long (ca. 3 ns) and few of them migrate to the octahedral cage exhibiting a stagnant H-bonding network in the tetrahedral cage. At high temperature, however, all cages are equally occupied by water molecules showing a longer H-bond network connected to two cages and a signicantly decreased residence time (ca. 125 ps). The connection of cages through the longer Hbond network offers a path for proton jumping from one cage to another. Therefore, the high conductivity of UiO-66(Zr)– $(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathbf{H})_{2}$ is attributed to the fast diffusivity over a long distance. The further in-depth exploration of MD simulation was carried out with dependence on the loading amounts of water molecules for the solvated proton structure, thermal dynamics and dynamics of hydrated protons in the 3D cage of UiO-66(Zr)– $(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{H})_{2}$ .84 As the water loading increases, $D_{\mathrm{s}}(\mathbf{CEC})$ and $D_{\mathrm{s}}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ present different trends (Fig. 24a). The former substantially increases with high loading of water corresponding to high conductivity. In contrast, $D_{\mathrm{s}}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)$ has a maximum at the intermediated loading and decreases with high loading, where the guest diffusion suffers from a predominant steric hindrance effect. In addition, RDF shows a rst peak around $2.5\substack{-2.6\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ which corresponds to the Zundel cation $(2.5\ \mathring\mathrm{A})$ . The small size (<3 A) of rst coordination sphere for pivot-hydronium ion means the decient solvation by water molecules. The average number of hydrogen bonds per water molecules (1.1, 1.5, 1.7, 2.0, and 2.15 for 20, 40, 50, 70, and $80\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/$ (unit cell, uc) is lower than that of bulk water (3.6),85 which can be attributed to the connement effect and hydrogen bonding with the $-\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ group. In addition, hydrogen bonding (HB) lifetimes $\left(\tau_{\mathrm{HB}}\right)$ decrease and increase sequentially as loading amounts increase (low (93 ps), intermediate (23 ps), and high water loading (52 ps)). This can be explained by the strong trapping by $-\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{H}$ at low water loading, favorable interactions with more freedom at intermediate loading, and the static HB network induced by steric hindrance at high loading. The residence time of water in the cage at high loading is approximately 1.5 ns due to slow translation and rotation motion by the static HB network (Fig. 24b). The evaluation of excess proton migration from one cage to another cage shows a distinct number of cages crossing depending on the level of water loading. The high loading facilitates proton migration toward at least three tetrahedral cages and four octahedral cages. Therefore, high relative humidity is benecial to create a conduction path between cages with high conductivity. Although MD simulation is benecial for fundamental understanding of the conduction mechanism at the molecular level, the related studies are still scarce. Future endeavors for computational study toward the various MOF structures and conducting media are required.
## 4. Conclusions and prospects
A H-bonded degenerate system of a conducting medium through acid–base chemistry is an interesting platform to design efficient solid-state proton conductors. In particular, $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and ${\bf N H}_{3}$ having an attractive conjugate acid–base system $\left(\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{O}^{+}–\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\right.$ , ${\bf O H}^{-}{\bf-H}_{2}{\bf O}_{2}$ and ${\bf N H_{4}}^{+}{-}{\bf N H}_{3}$ systems) are efficient conducting media because of their competent H-bonding ability. Recently, numerous reports have dealt with the proton conductivity of MOFs in various structural designs and strategies. In general, the proton conductivity of pristine MOFs is insignicant, whereas the incorporation of a proton conducting medium in a void space of MOFs considerably enhances the proton conductivity with the formation of a complete Hbonding network between the framework and the conned conducting medium. The major conducting medium of MOFs is water with additional acid molecules or functional groups for improved conductivity. In addition, ${\bf N H}_{3}$ as a conducting medium, which is either an alternative or a companion of water, has also attracted interest. The $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{3}}$ uptake in MOFs enhances the proton conduction properties and the coexistence with water facilitates protonation between the conducting media $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}–\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\right)$ . However, the structural stability of MOFs in the presence of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ with a high proton conductivity is still challenging and indispensable for practical applications in energy carriers with high-performance proton conduction. In particular, the intuitive understanding of the conduction mechanism in ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ mediated MOF proton conductors through the structural analysis is still scarce due to the structural stability problem.
To date, the investigation of proton conductivity in MOFs has focused on the discovery of a new proton-conducting pathway through the conducting medium; the scientic progress indicates a conductivity value comparable to that of Naon. The accumulated experimental knowledge suggests that (1) a hydrophilic channel or (2) acid species contained in the pore and framework and (3) defect and disordered sites are the major factors for high proton conductivity in MOFs. Moreover, the proton donation by acidic species to the conducting medium expedites the development of a degenerate proton-conduction system with increased conductivity. For practical applications of proton-conductive MOFs to FCs, future endeavors for bulk protonic conductivity, stability of MOFs in the redox reaction process, and computational mechanism analysis are required. In conclusion, with consideration of all challenges, the development of potential MOF materials for high proton conductivity should be directed toward the use of inert and oxophilic metal species with a low $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ functional group of ligands. Furthermore, the expansion of materials to organic and inorganic species for composites is expected to achieve practical protonconductive MOFs.
